{"id": "m247", "gold": "A", "step": "Pharmacology", "pred": "A", "response": "Cholinomimetic drugs mimic the action of acetylcholine and are used in conditions where there is a deficiency or blockade of cholinergic activity. They are not used in conditions where their action would be harmful or counterproductive.\n\n- **A. Bradycardia**: Cholinomimetics can slow the heart rate, so they are not used in bradycardia.\n- **B. Glaucoma**: Cholinomimetics (e.g., pilocarpine) are used in glaucoma to reduce intraocular pressure.\n- **C. Myasthenia gravis**: Cholinomimetics (e.g., neostigmine) are used to improve muscle strength in myasthenia gravis.\n- **D. Post-surgical atony or ileus**: Cholinomimetics can stimulate gastrointestinal motility, so they are used in post-surgical ileus.\n\nANSWER: A", "prompt_tokens": 107, "output_tokens": 193}
